def.p:
An ultra metric group is a topological group with a metrizable topology using a metric d that satisfies for all x,\,y, and z in the group,\begin{equation*}
 d(x,\,y)\leq \max (d(x,\,z),\,d(y,\,z)). 
\end{equation*}

sec.p:
Choose an element y\in X. If every element of y has support E then any nonempty proper subset of y has support E and therefore has support E\cup \{a\}. So we may assume that y has some element t such that E is not a support of t. Choose a finite non-empty subset F of A such that E\cap F=\emptyset and E\cup F is a support of t and such that F is minimal in the sense that for no proper subset F^{\prime } of F is E\cup F^{\prime } a support of t. We may assume without loss of generality that a\in F. (If a
ot \in F then we replace t by \psi (t) and F by \psi (F) where \psi is a permutation of A chosen so that \psi \in fix(E) and a\in \psi (F).) Let y'=\{\phi (t):\phi \in fix(E\cup \{a\})\}. Clearly y^{\prime } has support E\cup \{a\},\,y^{\prime }\subseteq y and y^{\prime }
eq \emptyset (since t\in y^{\prime }). It only remains to show that y^{\prime }
eq y.

