sec.p:
Fix \mathcal{A}=\{A_{i}:i\in \omega \} as in form 19, where the A_{i} are pairwise disjoint. Let  ar{A}_{i}=\{a_{i}\}\cup A_{i}\cup \{b_{i}\}, where a_{i},\,b_{i} are chosen so that a_{i},\, b_{i}
ot \in A_{i} and the  ar{A}_{i} are pairwise disjoint. Declare a_{i} to be less than every member of A_{i} and b_{i} to be greater than every member of A_{i}. Put X=\cup \{ ar{A}_{i}:i\in \omega \}. Order X by requiring: elements of  ar{A}_{i} are ordered in X the way they are ordered in  ar{A}_{i}, otherwise if x\in  ar{A}_{i} and y\in  ar{A}_{j} then x is less than y iff i<j. Put  ar{X}=X\cup \{a\},\,a
ot \in X and declare a to be greater than every member of X. Clearly  ar{X} with the order topology is a compact \mathrm{T}_{2} space and consequently a \mathrm{T}_{4} space. Furthermore, G=A(=\{a_{i}: i\in \omega \})\cup B(=\{b_{i}:i\in \omega \})\cup \{a\} is a closed subset of  ar{X} and the function f:Gightarrow [0,\,1] given by f(a)=0 and for i\in \omega \ f(a_{i})=1/(i+2), f(b_{i})=1/(i+1) is clearly continuous. Hence, f has a continuous extension  ar{f}:  ar{X}ightarrow [0,1]. Put f_{i}= ar{f}| ar{A}_{i}. As  ar{f} is continuous it follows that f_{i} is also continuous. Since  ar{A}_{i} is compact it follows that f_{i} attains both its minimum and maximum values. We may assume that f_{i} attains its minimum at a_{i}, its maximum at b_{i} and, at every other point of A_{i}, it takes on values other than f_{i}(a_{i}) and f_{i}(b_{i}). Let C_{i}=f_{i}^{-1}((y_{i},\,1/(1+i))) where y_{i}=  rac{2i+3}{2(i+1)(i+2)} is the midpoint of the interval [1/(2+i),\,1/(1+i)]. Clearly C_{i}
eq \emptyset and by the completeness of  ar{A}_{i} and the continuity of f_{i} it follows that c_{i}= \inf (C_{i})\in A_{i}. Hence, c=\{c_{i}:i\in \omega \} is a choice set for \mathcal{A}, finishing the proof of the theorem.

